WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.973
Picture a recipe card for pancakes.

2
00:00:04.973 --> 00:00:09.323
200 grams of flour, two eggs, 300 millilitres of milk, makes eight.

3
00:00:09.323 --> 00:00:14.842
Follow it with the same ingredients, and you get the same eight pancakes, every time.

4
00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:19.452
And the kitchen ends up the way you found it, apart from a pan to wash.

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.320
Now picture a second cook with the same card.

6
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.331
She glances at the clock, and makes half a batch, because it's late.

7
00:00:26.331 --> 00:00:29.810
She weighs the flour on a scale someone left set to ounces.

8
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.464
And she eats a spoonful of batter on the way.

9
00:00:32.464 --> 00:00:35.295
Same card, same ingredients, different pancakes.

10
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.638
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.559
My function has one parameter.

12
00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:42.992
How can it have more inputs than that?

13
00:00:42.992 --> 00:00:44.977
And why would a test even care?

14
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.146
Here's a small piece of a recipe app.

15
00:00:48.146 --> 00:00:52.881
Before you cook, it looks in the cupboard and tells you how much flour to buy.

16
00:00:52.881 --> 00:00:56.523
The shop only sells whole bags of one kilo, so it rounds up.

17
00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:57.773
Now look inside.

18
00:00:57.773 --> 00:01:03.490
It takes the bag from Cupboard, a global object that any part of the program can change.

19
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:09.596
It asks LocalDate.now() for today's date, and a bag past its best-before date counts as empty.

20
00:01:09.596 --> 00:01:12.325
And it writes a line on the shopping list.

21
00:01:12.325 --> 00:01:16.223
Two inputs and one output that the signature never mentions.

22
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:18.052
Now a test.

23
00:01:18.052 --> 00:01:24.904
The bag in the cupboard has 700 grams left, and it's good until the 31st of March.

24
00:01:24.904 --> 00:01:30.335
We need 1500 grams, so 800 are missing, and we buy one bag: 1000.

25
00:01:30.335 --> 00:01:34.931
The test expects 1000, and it passes all through March.

26
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:36.639
Nobody touches the code.

27
00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:39.776
What does the same test say on the first of April?

28
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:43.449
It fails.

29
00:01:43.449 --> 00:01:44.360
It got 2000.

30
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:51.338
On the first of April the bag is past its date, so it counts as empty, and we need two bags.

31
00:01:51.338 --> 00:01:53.007
The test didn't break.

32
00:01:53.007 --> 00:01:54.448
Its flour went off.

33
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:56.549
Order matters too.

34
00:01:56.549 --> 00:02:02.463
In the lesson's demo, a second test puts a new bag in the cupboard and never takes it out.

35
00:02:02.463 --> 00:02:07.917
Run the two tests in the other order, and the first one fails: expected 800, got 0.

36
00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:11.991
And the line that broke it isn't even in the test that failed.

37
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.712
The usual fixes add machinery.

38
00:02:14.712 --> 00:02:17.309
A fake clock that always says the same day.

39
00:02:17.309 --> 00:02:19.845
A reset of the cupboard before every test.

40
00:02:19.845 --> 00:02:21.476
Clearing the list after it.

41
00:02:21.476 --> 00:02:24.555
That's a lot of babysitting for one small function.

42
00:02:24.555 --> 00:02:27.937
Wouldn't it be nice to just hand it everything it needs?

43
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:31.460
Let's go back to the two cooks and map it.

44
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.004
The card is the function.

45
00:02:33.004 --> 00:02:35.473
The ingredients on it are its arguments.

46
00:02:35.473 --> 00:02:37.573
The pancakes are the return value.

47
00:02:37.573 --> 00:02:41.833
The clock, and the scale left on ounces, are inputs nobody passed in.

48
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:46.464
And eating the batter, like writing the shopping list, changes the kitchen.

49
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.078
Now we can give it a name.

50
00:02:49.078 --> 00:02:51.315
A pure function follows two rules.

51
00:02:51.315 --> 00:02:57.501
One: its result depends only on its arguments, so the same values always give the same answer.

52
00:02:57.501 --> 00:03:00.330
Two: calling it changes nothing outside it.

53
00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.357
Break either rule, and the function is impure.

54
00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:10.828
Anything a call does besides handing back its value is a side effect: writing a file, printing, changing a field.

55
00:03:10.828 --> 00:03:17.383
And anything it reads that can change, like the clock, a random number or a global, is a hidden input.

56
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.397
Let's try three.

57
00:03:19.397 --> 00:03:22.620
gramsFor turns cups into grams with a constant.

58
00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.118
isFresh compares a bag's date with LocalDate.now().

59
00:03:26.118 --> 00:03:30.644
And totalGrams sorts the list you gave it, then adds up the grams.

60
00:03:30.644 --> 00:03:32.221
Which of them are pure?

61
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.175
Only the first.

62
00:03:36.175 --> 00:03:38.970
isFresh reads the calendar, a hidden input.

63
00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:43.780
And totalGrams returns the right total, but it reorders the caller's list.

64
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.705
The answer is right, and it's still not pure.

65
00:03:46.705 --> 00:03:48.460
That one bites in practice.

66
00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:50.525
So here's the fix.

67
00:03:50.525 --> 00:03:53.162
Move every input into the parameter list.

68
00:03:53.162 --> 00:03:57.215
gramsToBuy takes the grams you need, the bag, and today's date.

69
00:03:57.215 --> 00:04:00.624
Nothing is read from outside, and nothing is written.

70
00:04:00.624 --> 00:04:04.227
The grams come back as the return value, and that's all.

71
00:04:04.433 --> 00:04:06.482
Now the test is plain values.

72
00:04:06.482 --> 00:04:12.415
The 31st of March gives 1000, the first of April gives 2000, two lines side by side.

73
00:04:12.415 --> 00:04:15.029
No fake clock, no reset, no clean up.

74
00:04:15.029 --> 00:04:23.788
Run them in any order, even on eight threads at once, that's eight jobs running at the same time, because nothing is shared.

75
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.188
The calendar can turn all it likes.

76
00:04:26.188 --> 00:04:28.126
This test brought its own date.

77
00:04:28.126 --> 00:04:33.940
So the point really is: a function that reads only its arguments is tested with plain values.

78
00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:37.341
The card picture breaks in one place.

79
00:04:37.341 --> 00:04:43.225
A real recipe never gives exactly the same pancakes twice, because eggs and pans differ.

80
00:04:43.225 --> 00:04:47.705
A pure function gives the same value every time, to the last digit.

81
00:04:47.705 --> 00:04:49.912
That's stronger than any kitchen.

82
00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:51.944
One honest limit.

83
00:04:51.944 --> 00:04:55.800
Kotlin has no pure keyword, and neither compiler checks it.

84
00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:04.230
The clues are in the signature: plain values in, a real return type instead of Unit or void, and no clock, file or logger inside.

85
00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:08.478
And a real app still has to read the cupboard and write the list.

86
00:05:08.478 --> 00:05:12.072
The end of the lesson pushes that work out to the edge.

87
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.507
So, how can a function have more inputs than parameters?

88
00:05:16.507 --> 00:05:19.075
By peeking, at the clock and at a global.

89
00:05:19.075 --> 00:05:22.269
A pure function doesn't peek, and it doesn't touch.

90
00:05:22.269 --> 00:05:23.459
Pass everything in.

91
00:05:23.667 --> 00:05:24.561
Quick check.

92
00:05:24.561 --> 00:05:30.003
portion divides the grams by four, and also adds one to a shared counter.

93
00:05:30.003 --> 00:05:30.897
Pure or not?

94
00:05:33.867 --> 00:05:34.477
Not pure.

95
00:05:34.477 --> 00:05:39.085
The portion is right, but bumping a shared counter is a side effect.
